export const guildGuides:GuildGuide[]=[
{
slug:'guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts',label:'Model Charts',title:'Guild Model Prefix Serial Number Charts',metaTitle:'Guild Model Prefix Serial Number Charts: 1965 To 1997',description:'Search Guild model-prefix serial charts from 1965 to 1997. Compare published ranges, annual last numbers, reused prefixes, missing records and overlaps.',intro:'Find your model first, then compare the complete serial. A range and an annual last-number entry answer different questions.',photo:'artistSerial',alt:'JA100123 serial stamped above the tuners of a Guild Artist Award',
sections:[
{id:'reading-the-charts',title:'How To Read A Guild Model Chart',html:`<p>Many Guild serial numbers are model-specific. During this period, refer to the model-specific prefixes to determine the year. The letters and digits must be compared with the correct model row; the digits don’t themselves encode the year.</p><p>A full range gives a published first and last number. An annual endpoint gives only the last number recorded for that model in that year. For example, the Artist Award ends 1984 at JA100122 and 1985 at JA100127. The photographed JA100123 sits between those adjacent records and supports a 1985 reading, assuming the instrument and sequence agree. A missing intervening year would weaken that inference.</p><p>Tip: the fastest way to navigate these model-specific charts on a computer is to press Ctrl+F and type your prefix into the search bar. You can then jump straight to the rows that share your guitar's prefix. The filter on this page also searches model names and serial cells. Clear it to restore every row.</p><p>These are the records published in <a href="${guildHistory}">Guild’s dating reference</a>. N/A means the table supplies no usable entry. A question mark, gap or repeated boundary stays visible because filling it in would invent data. For a plain numeric serial, return to the <a href="/guild-serial-number-lookup/">Guild serial lookup</a>. For a later import, read the <a href="/guild-vintage-vs-reissue/">series and country distinctions</a>.</p>`,photos:[pic('artistSerial','Guild Artist Award headstock stamped JA100123 and Made in U.S.A.','JA100123 falls after the published 1984 endpoint JA100122 and before the 1985 endpoint JA100127. It is a sequence comparison, not a year hidden in the digits.'),pic('artistFront','Sunburst Guild Artist Award archtop with a floating pickup','The Artist Award model identification belongs with the serial. A prefix lookup without the model can choose the wrong row.')]},
{id:'models-1965',title:'Model Ranges From 1965 To 1969',html:`<p>From 1960 to 1964, Guild continued a single sequential numbering system for all models. From 1965 to 1969, a separate serial system was introduced for each model. Your serial number may be a sequential one or a model-specific one, so check both charts and compare accordingly.</p><p>The AI prefix appears under both F30 and A150, so a prefix alone can give multiple answers. The F50 label <a href="/guild-labels-and-serial-number-locations/#read-the-whole-label">AD355</a> fits the F50 row in 1968. The printed Hoboken address is a separate piece of evidence. Don’t overwrite a model-specific result with an assumed factory date.</p><p>The source has gaps, including F30 numbers between AI341 and AI352 and a missing DC898 between Starfire XII entries. It also contains unusual standalone model labels such as BB195-241. They’re retained here as published, rather than silently assigned to another row.</p>`,table:'modelSpecific1965'},
{id:'models-1979',title:'Last Serial Numbers From 1979 To 1983',html:`<p>In late 1979, the prefix system was reintroduced. The figures below represent the last serial number produced for each model in each year. The <a href="/guild-serial-number-lookup/#part2">plain numeric 1979 chart</a> ends September 30; these model records cover the later system.</p><p>Compare adjacent years only when the model, prefix and sequence remain consistent. A blank year, a restarted count or a changed model name prevents a simple “between these two numbers” conclusion. Keep leading zeroes when you copy a serial.</p>`,table:'prefixes1979'},
{id:'models-1984',title:'Last Serial Numbers From 1984 To 1989',html:`<p>During this high-production era, Guild continued model-specific serialization. Match your model to find the last serial number of that year. Some model rows change prefix during the period. A shorter number in a newer sequence isn’t necessarily an older guitar.</p><p>Read both the model column and the full cell, including alternatives and question marks. If a worn stamp could be read two ways, take another <a href="/guild-labels-and-serial-number-locations/#photograph-a-faint-number">headstock photograph with side lighting</a> before comparing endpoints.</p>`,table:'westerly1984'},
{id:'models-1990',title:'Last Serial Numbers From 1990 To 1993',html:`<p>Guild continued model-specific serialization through 1994. This particular table ends in 1993; the 1994 endpoints appear in the next section. Related names such as D40 and D40C remain separate where the manufacturer publishes separate rows.</p><p>A model suffix can change the row you need. Copy cutaway, electronics and other suffixes from the actual instrument rather than abbreviating the name from memory. The <a href="/guild-acoustic-guitar-identification/">acoustic identification guide</a> explains which photographs help distinguish similar models.</p>`,table:'transition1990'},
{id:'models-1994',title:'1994 Endpoints And 1995 To 1996 Ranges',html:`<p>For 1994, factory records typically provide only the model prefix and the last serial number. For 1995 and 1996, Guild returned to providing full production ranges (first and last serial numbers) for each model. The table below preserves the manufacturer's record of this transition era.</p><p>Some published ranges overlap. For example, Artist Award AA000089 appears at the 1995 and 1996 boundary. That supports a transition-period answer until other evidence resolves it. Don’t adjust a boundary merely to make a calculator return one year.</p>`,table:'expansion1994'},
{id:'models-1997',title:'Published Ranges For 1997',html:`<p>As the 1990s progressed, Guild provided detailed beginning and ending serial-number ranges for each model. This chart covers 1997 production, including several well-known acoustics and the continuation of the Starfire electric series.</p><p>Guild’s source groups this material under a broader late-1990s tab, but the displayed table supplies 1997 records. It does not provide a complete 1998 or 1999 chart. A number beyond a listed range therefore needs further evidence, not an automatic next-year assignment. See the <a href="/guild-factory-eras/#corona">Corona transition discussion</a> for later instruments.</p>`,table:'late1997'}
],resources:[historyResource,faqResource,res(collection,'The Westerly Collection Announcement','Distinguish the modern collection name from the earlier Rhode Island factory.')]},
{
slug:'guild-labels-and-serial-number-locations',label:'Labels And Serials',title:'Guild Labels And Serial Number Locations',metaTitle:'Guild Serial Number Locations & Label Identification',description:'Find a Guild serial on the headstock, label or neck block. Compare close-up photos, model lines, Hoboken labels and modern import country markings.',intro:'Photograph the complete label and both sides of the headstock. The words around a serial can matter as much as the number.',photo:'f50Label',alt:'Guild F50 label with AD355 serial and Hoboken address',
sections:[
{id:'where-to-look',title:'Where To Find A Guild Serial Number',html:`<p>It depends on the era. Look on the back of the headstock, on a label inside the soundhole or body, or stamped on the neck block. Electric and acoustic models can differ, so check all three spots. An archtop label may be visible through an f-hole; an acoustic label is usually easier to photograph through the soundhole.</p><p>Start with the locations you can see without removing anything. Hold a small light outside the opening and change the angle until the model and serial lines are readable. Avoid putting adhesive, chalk or pencil on a stamp to darken it. If the label is loose, leave it in place and photograph its condition.</p><p>Use the <a href="/guild-serial-number-lookup/#gsl-tool">Guild decoder</a> after recording the full marking. Keep a typed transcription beside the photographs, including any uncertain character. A close-up of four digits without the model or prefix can send you to the wrong chart.</p>`,photos:[pic('f48Label','Oval Guild guarantee label with F48 model and serial 74329','The F48 model and 74329 serial appear on separate lines. The serial falls within Guild’s published 1972 numeric range.'),pic('f48Serial','74329 stamped on the rear of the same Guild F48 headstock','A second serial location helps verify the transcription. Record both when the instrument has both.')]},
{id:'read-the-whole-label',title:'Read The Model, Address And Serial Separately',html:`<p>The F50 label below reads AD355 and carries a Hoboken, New Jersey address. In <a href="${guildHistory}">Guild’s model records</a>, AD292 through AD355 belong to F50 production in 1968. Guild’s <a href="${collection}">Westerly history</a> dates the relocation to 1967. Those facts show why a printed address should not be treated as a precise assembly-date stamp.</p><p>The F30 example is different: its label and headstock carry 29030, a plain number in the 1964 range. The model line reads F30, followed by a finish notation. The 30 in F30 is not a year, and the letters beside the model shouldn’t be merged with the serial.</p><p>Paper color is a weak shortcut. Aged white stock, warm lighting and a yellowed finish can all make a label appear tan. Read its wording and layout before comparing the <a href="/guild-factory-eras/">factory eras</a>. The <a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/#models-1965">1965 to 1969 model chart</a> is the right next step for the F50 example.</p>`,photos:[pic('f50Label','F50 Guild guarantee label with serial AD355 and printed Hoboken address','AD355 is the last number listed for F50 in 1968. The Hoboken address does not turn that model-chart result into an earlier year.'),pic('f30Label','Guild F30 label with handwritten serial 29030','This F30 uses a plain numeric serial. Copy the model and finish notation separately from 29030.')]},
{id:'electric-labels',title:'Labels Inside Archtops And Hollowbody Electrics',html:`<p>A narrow f-hole can hide part of a label even when the guitar is well lit. Take one photograph showing the label in the body and another aimed through the opening. The wider view establishes where the label sits; the closer view records its writing. Don’t fill in a hidden digit from a seller’s date estimate.</p><p>The earlier X-175 example has white pickup covers and a short numeric headstock stamp. The later example has metal-covered pickups and a partly obscured paper label. Use the <a href="/guild-electric-guitar-identification/">electric model guide</a> to compare the whole instrument rather than assuming every X-175 shares one serial format.</p><p>If the paper is missing, describe it as missing and continue with the headstock, construction and repair history. A lost label limits the evidence; it doesn't by itself establish a counterfeit or prove a particular year.</p>`,photos:[pic('earlyXLabel','Guild label partly visible through the f-hole of an early sunburst X175','The f-hole hides portions of this label. A different angle is needed before transcribing every character.'),pic('laterXLabel','Paper label through the f-hole of a later Guild X175','Photograph the surrounding body and pickup along with the label to keep the identification context.')]},
{id:'photograph-a-faint-number',title:'Photograph A Faint Headstock Stamp',html:`<p>Try daylight from the side rather than flash straight at the finish. Take a square-on frame and a second frame with the light crossing the stamped grooves. Keep the entire headstock in the first image, then move closer for the number. A sharp original file is more useful than a digitally sharpened screenshot.</p><p>The early X-175 stamp below reads 11830, which fits the approximate late-1950s sequence. The F30 reads 29030, which fits 1964. Those are two different examples, so their tuner arrangements should not be combined into one imagined specification.</p><p>Record any plugged screw holes, finish disturbance or tuner changes without removing the hardware. The <a href="/guild-pickup-and-hardware-dating-guide/#tuners-and-headstocks">tuner and headstock comparisons</a> explain what those details can add to a serial reading.</p>`,photos:[pic('earlyXSerial','Rear headstock of an early Guild X175 stamped 11830','Side lighting makes a shallow number easier to read. 11830 falls in the approximate 1959 portion of the early records.'),pic('f30Serial','Guild F30 rear headstock stamped 29030 above open-gear tuners','29030 falls in the 1964 numeric range. The open-gear tuners are additional inspection evidence, not a separate date code.')]},
{id:'modern-country-markings',title:'Modern Labels And Country Markings',html:`<p>Joe’s <a href="${d150}">D-150NAT listing</a> shows a Westerly Collection label and a separate serial marking that reads G1150733, Made in China. The word Westerly here is the collection name. It does not mean the guitar was built at the old Rhode Island factory.</p><p>Keep the model label and country mark together when you ask for help. This example should not be run through the short numeric 1960s table or the Tacoma Julian system. The <a href="/guild-vintage-vs-reissue/">vintage versus reissue guide</a> explains why familiar names and logos return on later instruments.</p><p>If the number remains unresolved, send the complete front and back, headstock, model label and serial to a <a href="/free-appraisal/guild/">Guild appraisal</a>. Include repairs and ownership history you know, without guessing at missing details.</p>`,photos:[pic('d150Label','Westerly Collection model label inside Joe’s Guild D150NAT','The modern collection name honors Guild history. It is not an old factory address.',d150),pic('d150Serial','G1150733 serial label with Made in China wording inside a Guild D150NAT','The country marking is clear. The vintage U.S. serial charts do not decode this example.',d150)]}
],resources:[historyResource,faqResource,res(collection,'Guild’s Westerly Collection Introduction','Read the manufacturer’s explanation of the modern series and its historical name.')]},
{
slug:'guild-factory-eras',label:'Factory Eras',title:'Guild Factory Eras And Serial Number Systems',metaTitle:'Guild Factory Eras: Hoboken, Westerly, Tacoma & More',description:'Compare Guild’s New York, Hoboken, Westerly, Corona, Tacoma, New Hartford and Oxnard eras, with serial examples and guidance for imported models.',intro:'A factory name helps choose a serial system. It does not replace the model, the full number or a physical inspection.',photo:'f50Label',alt:'Hoboken address on a Guild F50 label with AD355 serial',
sections:[
{id:'new-york-and-hoboken',title:'New York And Hoboken: The Early Instruments',html:`<p>Guild’s early story includes New York before Hoboken. That matters when a short numeric serial is advertised simply as a “Hoboken-era” guitar. The <a href="/guild-serial-number-lookup/#part1">1953 to 1959 records</a> are approximate; the 1960 to 1964 chart supplies clearer first and last numbers.</p><p>On the photographed F30, the label says Hoboken and the headstock reads 29030. That combination is consistent with the 1964 chart. On the F50, the label also says Hoboken, but AD355 belongs to the 1968 F50 row. A printed address can therefore remain useful without giving a precise assembly location.</p><p>For an early guitar, photograph the label wording, headstock front and back, body shape and hardware. Compare the <a href="/guild-labels-and-serial-number-locations/">label examples</a> before deciding which chart applies. An address copied from an old label is not enough to resolve a transition instrument.</p>`,photos:[pic('f30Front','Guild F30 acoustic from Joe’s archive with plain numeric serial 29030','The F30 example combines a Hoboken label with a serial in the 1964 range.'),pic('f50Label','Hoboken address and AD355 serial on a Guild F50 label','This model-chart example dates to 1968 despite the printed Hoboken address.')]},
{id:'westerly',title:'Westerly: Model Prefixes And Numeric Sequences',html:`<p>Guild’s <a href="${collection}">own Westerly retrospective</a> places the relocation to Rhode Island in 1967. The factory era and serial era do not begin on the same day: the model-specific charts cover 1965 to 1969, spanning the relocation.</p><p>In 1970, Guild consolidated production back into a single sequential system. Dating Guilds from this period is much easier than the preceding era. Just match your number to the year ranges in the <a href="/guild-serial-number-lookup/#part2">1970 to 1979 chart</a>. The F48 serial 74329, for example, falls in 1972.</p><p>Later Westerly instruments use model records again. The Artist Award JA100123 is compared with annual endpoints, while the 1995 to 1997 material includes explicit ranges. Those differences explain why there isn't one “Westerly formula.” Use the <a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/">full model charts</a> with the model printed on the instrument.</p>`,photos:[pic('f48Front','Natural Guild F48 acoustic with black pickguard','The F48 serial 74329 is a numeric-sequence example from 1972.'),pic('artistSerial','JA100123 headstock serial on a Guild Artist Award','Later model prefixes require the relevant model row and year-by-year records.')]},
{id:'corona',title:'Corona: The Late-2001 Transition',html:`<p>The <a href="${faq}">Guild FAQ</a> describes the late-2001 move to Corona, California, including nearly complete Westerly kits finished there. That can complicate a simple choice between “Westerly” and “Corona.” Record what the instrument and documents actually say rather than forcing a transition guitar into a single label.</p><p>Guild identifies C-leading serial prefixes for Corona production. But C is not a universal Corona test: older Mark-series records include prefixes such as CA and CB. A nylon-string Mark model with a 1960s prefix should not become a 2000s guitar merely because its number begins with C.</p><p>The published charts on this site don't provide a complete universal 1998 to 2004 decoder. For an unresolved example, gather the model, full serial, label, country marking and any original paperwork. The <a href="/guild-acoustic-guitar-identification/">acoustic identification guide</a> helps separate a model designation from the serial.</p>`},
{id:'tacoma-and-new-hartford',title:'Tacoma And New Hartford: Julian Date Codes',html:`<p>Guild’s <a href="${guildHistory}">dating notes</a> give February 2005 for the Tacoma system and December 2008 for New Hartford. These serials use a factory letter, a year letter, a three-digit day of year and a three-digit unit sequence. TJ037016 reads February 6, 2006 in the Tacoma format; NM016002 reads January 16, 2009 in the New Hartford format.</p><p>The day field is a calendar count, not a month and day stuck together. Day 060 is March 1 in a common year and February 29 in a leap year. Day 366 is valid only in a leap year. The <a href="/guild-serial-number-lookup/#gsl-tool">decoder</a> checks those calendar limits rather than accepting an impossible date.</p><p>Factory timing also matters. An N-prefixed reading before December 2008 contradicts the stated start of New Hartford production. A letter that would produce a much later year should not be extended indefinitely into Oxnard production. Use the full factory context alongside the arithmetic.</p>`},
{id:'oxnard-and-imports',title:'Oxnard And Imported Guild Lines',html:`<p>Later USA production moved to Oxnard, California. The <a href="https://guildguitars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/AG282_guild.pdf">2016 factory visit published in Acoustic Guitar</a> describes the operating factory and January 2016 relaunch. An ownership change, a factory buildout and the date a particular guitar was made are different milestones.</p><p>Imported instruments have their own model and serial context. The D-150NAT below carries a Chinese country marking and a Westerly Collection label. Its series name refers to Guild heritage. It isn't evidence of manufacture in Rhode Island.</p><p>For a current model, check the manufacturer's specification for the exact version. A USA M-20, an imported acoustic and a Newark St. electric can share familiar Guild design details without sharing a serial algorithm. Compare the <a href="/guild-vintage-vs-reissue/">vintage and later examples</a>, then include the country mark in any <a href="/free-appraisal/guild/">appraisal request</a>.</p>`,photos:[pic('d150Front','Natural Guild D150NAT acoustic from Joe’s Reverb listing','This later acoustic is identified separately from the vintage Westerly factory instruments.',d150),pic('d150Serial','Guild D150 serial marking with Made in China wording','The country marking helps choose the correct production context.',d150)]}
],resources:[faqResource,res(collection,'Guild’s Westerly Retrospective','Check the stated 1967 relocation and the later collection’s name.'),historyResource,res('https://guildguitars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/AG282_guild.pdf','Oxnard Factory Visit, 2016','Read the eyewitness account of operating production and the January 2016 relaunch.')]},
{
slug:'guild-acoustic-guitar-identification',label:'Acoustic Models',title:'Guild Acoustic Guitar Identification Guide',metaTitle:'Guild Acoustic Identification: Models, Labels & Features',description:'Identify Guild acoustic guitars by body, model name, labels and construction. Compare original features and photographs before checking the serial number.',intro:'Start with the model line, then compare the body and appointments. Similar-looking Guild acoustics can belong to different models and factory periods.',photo:'f50Front',alt:'Natural Guild F50 acoustic with ornate headstock and block inlays',
sections:[
{id:'model-and-body',title:'Match The Model Line To The Body',html:`<p>Read the model inside the body before naming an acoustic from its outline. The F30, F48 and F50 examples below have related Guild styling but different proportions and appointments. Their model names are not production years, and an F prefix should not be treated as one unchanging body size across Guild history.</p><p>Write down the complete designation, including finish, cutaway or electronics suffixes. Then record the serial separately. On the F30 label, 29030 is the serial; on the F50, AD355 is the serial. Use the <a href="/guild-serial-number-lookup/">serial lookup</a> to put those instruments into a production period before comparing specifications.</p><p>Take a straight front photograph with the entire body and headstock visible. An angled view can make the lower bout look wider or hide the shoulder shape. A back photograph helps document wood appearance and construction, but grain alone won't prove the species or whether every panel is solid wood.</p>`,photos:[pic('f30Front','Front view of a Guild F30 acoustic with tortoiseshell-pattern pickguard','The F30 label and 29030 serial belong with this body shape and trim.'),pic('f50Front','Front view of a Guild F50 acoustic with block fingerboard inlays','The F50 example carries more elaborate appointments. Its model line, not the amount of decoration, identifies it.')]},
{id:'headstock-and-inlays',title:'Headstock Logos And Fingerboard Inlays',html:`<p>The F48 has the Guild name with a crown and vertical decorative inlay. The F50 shown here uses a shield-style emblem below the name. These are useful visible differences between the examples, but neither logo alone supplies an exact year.</p><p>Compare the inlay shapes, their fret positions, the headstock outline and the truss rod cover. A replaced cover is easier to change than an inlaid logo, yet even an old-looking logo can appear on a later model. Use several features together with the <a href="/guild-factory-eras/">factory and serial context</a>.</p><p>A clear fingerboard photograph also records fret wear, replacement frets and binding condition. Those details matter to an appraisal even when they don't narrow the year. Don't confuse the decorative material's color in a warm photograph with a precise material identification.</p>`,photos:[pic('f48Head','Guild F48 headstock with crown and vertical inlay','Record the full arrangement of name, emblem, cover and tuners.'),pic('f50Head','Guild F50 headstock with shield emblem','This shield-style design differs from the F48 example; the serial still does the dating work.'),pic('f48Inlays','Rectangular fingerboard inlays on a Guild F48','Fingerboard markers should be photographed along the whole neck.'),pic('f50Back','Figured maple back of Joe’s Guild F50','Back and side photographs add useful model and condition evidence beyond a front view.')]},
{id:'bridge-and-neck-condition',title:'Bridges, Saddles And Neck Condition',html:`<p>Photograph the bridge from above and from the side. The overhead view records its shape, pin layout and surrounding finish. The side view shows the saddle and whether a gap is visible at the bridge edge. A photograph can show a possible problem, but it cannot establish the condition of the bridge plate inside the guitar.</p><p>The F30 close-up shows wear around the top and pickguard. Preserve those details in an appraisal photograph instead of polishing them away. On the F48, the low viewing angle records the bridge and string path. Describe what is visible without declaring a neck reset necessary from a single image.</p><p>If the action is high or the saddle unusually low, send measurements with the photos. A <a href="/repair/">guitar repair assessment</a> can distinguish setup needs from structural work. The cost and extent of repairs also affect which examples make sense for a <a href="/post/blue-book-of-guitar-values-and-vintage-guitar-price-guide/">value comparison</a>.</p>`,photos:[pic('f30Bridge','Guild F30 bridge and worn pickguard area','Document top wear and the bridge outline before any cleaning or repair.'),pic('f48Bridge','Low-angle view across the Guild F48 acoustic bridge','A side view adds information about the saddle and bridge edge that a front photograph misses.'),pic('f50Bridge','Guild F50 bridge, saddle and six bridge pins','Keep bridge shape, pin placement and nearby finish in the same frame.'),pic('f50Heel','Guild F50 body edge, binding and neck heel area','An oblique view can help record binding condition and the neck-to-body area.')]},
{id:'twelve-string-and-later-models',title:'Twelve-Strings And Later Model Names',html:`<p>Count the strings and tuners, then copy the complete model. Guild’s historical charts include F112, F212, F312, F412 and F512 rows. Similar numbers do not make those instruments interchangeable, and a model name reused later doesn't bring its old serial scheme with it.</p><p>For a twelve-string, photograph both sides of the headstock, the bridge, the neck heel and the top around the bridge. Include the tuning and string gauge if you know them. A repair history is particularly useful when assessing neck angle, bridge work or a repaired headstock.</p><p>The modern <a href="${acoustics}">Guild acoustic catalog</a> organizes current models by shape and series. Use it for the later model you're examining, rather than applying current naming rules backward to every vintage F-series instrument. The <a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/">historical model tables</a> retain the older designations.</p>`,photos:[pic('f30Neck','Rear neck and heel of a Guild F30','A complete neck and heel view belongs in an acoustic identification set.'),pic('d150Front','Modern Guild D150NAT dreadnought with natural finish','A later model needs its own specification and country context, even when its styling looks familiar.',d150)]},
{id:'acoustic-appraisal-photos',title:'Photos For An Acoustic Guild Appraisal',html:`<p>Send the front, back, both sides, headstock front and rear, label and bridge. Add close-ups of cracks, binding separation, overspray or repaired areas. Keep the original resolution so a stamp or label can be enlarged.</p><p>Include the case and any receipts, but don't use the case's appearance as proof of the guitar's date. Cases and tuners can be replaced during decades of ownership. The <a href="/guild-pickup-and-hardware-dating-guide/">hardware guide</a> explains how to describe those changes separately.</p><p>A <a href="/free-appraisal/guild/">free Guild appraisal</a> considers the model, date evidence, condition and originality together. If you're ready to sell, the <a href="/sell-my-guild-guitar/">Guild buying page</a> explains how to send those details to Joe.</p>`}
],resources:[historyResource,res(acoustics,'Guild’s Current Acoustic Models','Compare current body-shape and series names with the model actually on your instrument.'),res(manuals,'Guild Electronics Manuals','Identify installed acoustic pickup and preamp systems without assuming they date the guitar.')]},
{
slug:'guild-electric-guitar-identification',label:'Electric Models',title:'Guild Electric Guitar Identification Guide',metaTitle:'Guild Electric Identification: Starfire, X-175 & More',description:'Compare Guild Starfire, X-175, X-500, Artist Award & Thunderbird photos. Check body construction, pickups, controls, serials and later reissue differences.',intro:'Identify the body and model before dating the parts. Guild archtops, Starfires and solidbody designs use different construction and serial records.',photo:'earlyXFront',alt:'Sunburst early Guild X175 with white pickup covers and harp tailpiece',
sections:[
{id:'body-and-model',title:'Start With The Body And Model',html:`<p>A single cutaway, two f-holes and two pickups are not enough to identify every Guild archtop. Record the model label, body depth, cutaway outline, pickup arrangement and tailpiece. If a label is partly hidden, use the <a href="/guild-labels-and-serial-number-locations/#electric-labels">f-hole photography examples</a> before guessing the model.</p><p>The X-175 and X-500 below show different levels of trim and hardware. The natural X-500 has gold-colored hardware and a Bigsby; that photograph alone doesn't establish when the vibrato was installed. The model's identity, its manufacture date and its present equipment should be recorded separately.</p><p>After identifying the model, compare the complete number with the <a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/">model-prefix charts</a> or the <a href="/guild-serial-number-lookup/#part1">early numeric records</a>. Don't choose a year solely because another guitar with a similar finish was advertised that way.</p>`,photos:[pic('earlyXFront','Sunburst Guild X175 with white pickups and harp-shaped tailpiece','The early X-175 example has white pickup covers, four knobs and a harp-shaped tailpiece.'),pic('x500Front','Natural Guild X500 archtop with gold-colored hardware and Bigsby','Photograph the Bigsby mounting area before making an originality claim. The installed hardware does not date the body by itself.')]},
{id:'x175-comparison',title:'Compare Two X-175 Examples',html:`<p>The earlier X-175 has white pickup covers and a headstock stamped 11830. The later archive example has metal-covered pickups and different visible appointments. These photographs make a useful comparison without turning one component change into a universal model-year boundary.</p><p>Look at the pickup surrounds, bridge, guard attachment, selector position and knob layout. Then compare the headstock emblem and fingerboard markers. A mixed set of details may reflect repairs or replacements; it needs an explanation rather than an automatic rejection of the serial.</p><p>The back of the later example shows contrasting neck laminations and the heel area. Include that view when assessing a repair. A finish line, grain change or reflection is not enough on its own to diagnose a broken neck. Use the <a href="/guild-pickup-and-hardware-dating-guide/">hardware guide</a> for closer pickup and tuner comparisons.</p>`,photos:[pic('earlyXPickups','White pickup covers and control layout on an early Guild X175','This front detail records the pickups, guard, bridge and controls together.'),pic('laterXPickups','Metal-covered pickups and bridge on a later Guild X175','Compare the installed equipment with the model and serial, allowing for replacements.'),pic('earlyXInlays','Block fingerboard inlays on an early Guild X175','Inlay shape and placement add model context to the pickup comparison.'),pic('laterXNeck','Rear neck laminations and heel area of a Guild X175','The neck and heel view is useful for construction and repair documentation.')]},
{id:'starfire-family',title:'Starfire Models Need Their Full Designation',html:`<p>Starfire is a family name. A roman numeral, bass designation or later suffix can change the model you are comparing. Write down the complete name and inspect whether the guitar has one or two cutaways, its body depth and its pickup and control arrangement.</p><p>The photographed Starfire III has a single cutaway, two pickups and a vibrato. These features help describe the instrument, but a natural-looking finish doesn't prove a factory natural finish. Refinishing and replacement hardware need separate evidence, such as a repair record, old photographs or an inspection.</p><p>Current Starfire models revisit earlier designs. Guild’s <a href="${press}">manufacturer press material</a> describes later Newark St. versions with modern changes. Use the <a href="/guild-vintage-vs-reissue/#newark-street">reissue comparison</a> before treating the word Starfire as evidence of 1960s production.</p>`,photos:[pic('starfireFront','Single-cutaway Guild Starfire III with two pickups and vibrato','Record the exact Starfire model, body shape and hardware. The photograph does not establish the finish’s originality.'),pic('starfireHead','Guild Starfire III headstock with crown and vertical inlay','A familiar headstock design is one clue among the label, serial and construction.')]},
{id:'artist-award',title:'Artist Award And Larger Archtops',html:`<p>The Artist Award example has a floating pickup near the neck, an ornate headstock and a sunburst archtop body. A floating pickup is mounted differently from pickups set through the top. That distinction matters when comparing an instrument with a model specification or assessing later modifications.</p><p>Its JA100123 headstock number belongs to the Artist Award annual-endpoint records. The <a href="/guild-model-prefix-serial-number-charts/#reading-the-charts">worked chart example</a> explains the 1984 and 1985 comparison. Read every letter and zero; JA100123 and a shorter AA-prefix number belong to different portions of the records.</p><p>Photograph the pickup attachment, pickguard, bridge base, tailpiece and top beneath the strings. Those areas can document equipment changes without removing parts. For a value opinion, include any history of a refret, neck work or top repair.</p>`,photos:[pic('artistFront','Guild Artist Award archtop with floating pickup and sunburst finish','The pickup mounting and ornate appointments help identify the model alongside its serial.'),pic('artistHead','Ornate Guild Artist Award headstock and truss rod cover','Record the headstock decoration and cover as part of the whole instrument.')]},
{id:'thunderbird-and-bluesbird',title:'Thunderbird And Bluesbird Features',html:`<p>The Thunderbird has a distinctive offset body, a crowded control area and unusual hardware. The back view shows the built-in support mechanism on this example. Photograph those features directly rather than relying on a name attached to a body silhouette.</p><p>A Bluesbird can also carry changed hardware. Joe’s <a href="${bluesbird}">1970s black Bluesbird listing</a> explicitly describes an added Bigsby and visible old bridge holes. That is a useful reminder that a branded vibrato can be a later installation on a vintage guitar.</p><p>Use the <a href="/guild-pickup-and-hardware-dating-guide/#bridges-and-vibratos">bridge and vibrato guide</a> to document the installation. If you’re preparing to <a href="/sell-my-guild-guitar/">sell a vintage Guild</a>, list known changes with the photographs so the date and originality can be assessed separately.</p>`,photos:[pic('thunderFront','Sunburst Guild Thunderbird with offset body and multiple controls','The Thunderbird’s shape and control arrangement distinguish it from Guild’s archtops.'),pic('thunderBack','Back of a Guild Thunderbird showing its support mechanism','Rear hardware and mounting points belong in an identification photo set.'),pic('bluesbirdFront','Black Guild Bluesbird with an added Bigsby from Joe’s listing','Joe’s listing discloses the added Bigsby. Its presence is not evidence of factory installation.',bluesbird)]}
],resources:[historyResource,res(press,'Guild’s Reissue And Product Announcements','Compare later Starfire and other revived designs with their historical references.'),res(bluesbird,'Joe’s Modified Bluesbird Example','Read the disclosed Bigsby addition and visible mounting evidence.')]},
{
slug:'guild-pickup-and-hardware-dating-guide',label:'Pickups And Hardware',title:'Guild Pickup And Hardware Dating Guide',metaTitle:'Guild Pickup & Hardware Dating: Types, Eras And Models',description:'Identify Guild pickups, bridges, tailpieces and tuners by model and era. Compare construction details and photos before deciding which parts are original.',intro:'Hardware can support a Guild identification, but it can also have been replaced. Describe the installed parts before deciding what they prove. The charts below name the pickups and hardware, with typical eras and model examples.',photo:'thunderControls',alt:'Close-up of Guild Thunderbird pickups and controls',
sections:[
pickupEraSection,
{id:'pickup-identification',title:'Compare Pickup Faces And Mounting',html:`<p>Start with a straight-on photograph of each pickup. Record the cover shape, number of visible pole pieces, mounting screws and surround. The earlier X-175 shows broad white Franz single coils. The later example has compact Guild Anti-Hum humbuckers, consistent with the 1960s specification. Compare their width, pole layout and mounting arrangement before grouping them with the larger HB-1.</p><p>Don't identify a pickup's internals solely from its cover. Covers and complete assemblies can be replaced, and later reproductions may imitate an older appearance. If a repairer has already removed a pickup, clear photographs of its underside and wiring can add evidence. There is no need to dismantle a fragile guitar simply to complete a photo checklist.</p><p>Compare the installed pair with the exact model and period in the <a href="/guild-electric-guitar-identification/">electric identification guide</a>. A serial dates the instrument's record; it doesn't prove that every part was installed at the factory.</p>`,photos:[pic('earlyXPickups','White Franz single-coil pickups on an early Guild X175','Broad Franz covers and a central row of adjustable pole screws distinguish this early pickup family. The pickup face, surrounding top and mounting arrangement should be photographed together.'),pic('laterXPickups','Compact Guild Anti-Hum humbuckers on an X175','The compact Anti-Hum cover and mounting pattern agree with the 1960s catalog illustration. This is distinct from the later, larger HB-1. A different cover is a reason to compare model specifications, not an automatic exact-year test.')]},
modernPickupSection,
{id:'controls-and-pot-codes',title:'Controls And Potentiometer Codes',html:`<p>The Thunderbird control area includes more switches than a typical two-pickup archtop. Photograph the entire arrangement, including the guard and labels, before naming each control. A missing switch, changed knob or extra hole can affect the originality description even if the circuit still works.</p><h3>Stove Knobs And Guild Logo Knobs</h3><p>The tall black knobs often called stove knobs occur on late-1950s X-50, X-150 and X-175 examples, as recorded in the <a href="${guildHardwareSources.franz}">X-50 inspection</a>. Later black skirted knobs with metal Guild-logo tops appear on the Thunderbird photographed here and in the <a href="${guildHardwareSources.catalog1978}#page=11">1978 electric catalog</a>. These give a period comparison, but a knob is easily replaced and its logo doesn’t identify the guitar’s year.</p><h3>Read The Pot Code Alongside The Harness</h3><p>For a seven-digit EIA pot code, the first three digits identify the maker, such as 137 for CTS. The next two digits identify the year, and the last two the week of production. Six-digit codes can leave the decade ambiguous. The <a href="/fender-pot-codes/">pot-code guide</a> explains the shared component-dating method with additional examples.</p><p>A pot code dates that component, not the completed Guild. Parts may have sat in inventory before assembly or been installed during a later repair. Read the code alongside solder work and the rest of the harness if a qualified repairer already has access. Don't pull a hollowbody harness through an f-hole just to chase an exact year.</p>`,photos:[pic('thunderControls','Guild Thunderbird with narrow white single coils, Hagstrom vibrato and Guild-logo knobs','The narrow Guild single coils, often called Mickey Mouse pickups, sit beside black Guild-logo knobs and the separate Hagstrom bridge and vibrato. This view records the control layout and hardware as a group. Individual controls can be changed over time.'),pic('thunderSerial','Serial number stamped above the tuners on a Guild Thunderbird','Keep the instrument’s serial evidence separate from dates found on electrical components.')]},
{id:'tuners-and-headstocks',title:'Tuners And Headstock Details',html:`<p>Photograph both faces of the headstock and the tuner backs. Look at the tuner housings, buttons, bushings and screw positions. Unused holes, outlines in the finish or mismatched screws can suggest a changed set, but they need a close inspection before a definite conclusion.</p><p>The natural X-500 example shows ornate covered Kolb tuners with pearloid buttons. The early X-175 uses a different arrangement. Their differences are useful for documenting the examples; they aren't a universal timeline that applies to every Guild model.</p><p>Logos and headstock emblems also vary by model and can recur on later instruments. Compare the shield, crown and Thunderbird bird designs with the <a href="/guild-labels-and-serial-number-locations/">serial and label</a>, then check the <a href="/guild-vintage-vs-reissue/">later-production context</a> where needed.</p>${tunerEraTable}<p>The <a href="${guildHardwareSources.catalog1976}#page=20">1976 acoustic catalog</a> lists Grover, Schaller and Guild machines across the flat-top range. That overlap is why a brand name alone can’t separate all 1970s acoustics into exact years. Match the model specification and screw pattern, including any traces of a previous set.</p>`,photos:[pic('x500Tuners','Gold-colored Kolb-pattern tuners on the rear of a Guild X500 headstock','The decorative covered housings and pearloid buttons identify the Kolb pattern. Record the housings, buttons and screw locations before deciding whether the set has changed.'),pic('earlyXSerial','Early Guild X175 headstock showing serial 11830 and tuner backs','The serial and tuner arrangement share a frame, but each supplies different evidence.'),pic('x500Head','Guild X500 headstock with shield emblem and decorative inlays','The headstock decoration helps distinguish the model’s trim.'),pic('thunderHead','Guild Thunderbird headstock with bird decoration','The Thunderbird emblem identifies a design feature; it does not independently date the instrument.')]},
{id:'bridges-and-vibratos',title:'Bridges, Tailpieces And Added Bigsbys',html:`<p>Look at how the tailpiece attaches, the position of the bridge and any unused mounting holes. A Guild-branded vibrato can be original equipment on one example and a later addition on another. The brand on the metal is not a record of its installation date.</p><p>Joe’s <a href="${bluesbird}">Bluesbird listing</a> states that its Bigsby was added and that the old bridge holes and ground wire remain visible. Keep that disclosure with the photograph. A later part doesn't change the guitar’s build date, but it does change an “all original” description.</p><p>On an acoustic, inspect the bridge outline and surrounding finish for a changed footprint. On an archtop, a movable bridge base can shift without a permanent modification. Describe the mounting evidence before assuming that every unusual bridge position means the guitar was rebuilt.</p>${hardwareEraTable}<p>A useful model-specific distinction appears in <a href="${guildHardwareSources.starfireFeatures}">Starfire IV research</a>: early-1970s examples used a Hagstrom bridge on a floating wood base, while later examples used a Muller bridge on body-mounted studs. The approximate change is around 1971. Check the base and mounting method as well as the saddle shape.</p>`,photos:[pic('bluesbirdBigsby','Added Guild-branded Bigsby on Joe’s black Bluesbird','The original listing identifies this as an addition. Branding alone cannot establish factory installation.',bluesbird),pic('f50Bridge','Bridge and saddle on a Guild F50 acoustic','Bridge shape and the finish around its footprint can help a repairer assess previous work.')]},
acousticPickupSection,
{id:'finish-binding-and-repairs',title:'Finish, Binding And Repair Evidence',html:`<p>Vintage Guilds used nitrocellulose lacquer, which often develops fine “weather checking” lines over time. Checking is useful condition information, but a later finish can also check. Its presence does not prove an untouched factory finish.</p><p>Binding can shrink, separate or be repaired. A small gap needs a close photograph, not a conclusion that the guitar is either original or beyond repair. Compare the neck heel, headstock joints, body edges and any areas where the color or surface texture changes.</p><p>The later X-175 neck view records the laminations and finish around the heel. The early X-175 headstock records a different logo and tuner arrangement. Keep each instrument’s evidence together. For an <a href="/free-appraisal/guild/">appraisal</a>, list known repairs and send full views as well as close-ups. The article on <a href="/post/what-a-serial-number-cant-tell-you/">what a serial number cannot tell you</a> explains why condition and originality need their own assessment.</p>`,photos:[pic('laterXNeck','Back of Guild X175 neck with contrasting laminations','Document finish and construction details without diagnosing a repair from one reflection.'),pic('earlyXHead','Front of an early Guild X175 headstock','Headstock photos support model and condition checks alongside the serial.')]}
],resources:[res(guildHardwareSources.catalog1978,'Guild’s 1978 Electric Catalog','Read the S-300 pickup options, named bridges and archtop hardware specifications.'),res(guildHardwareSources.catalog1976,'Guild’s 1976 Acoustic Catalog','See the factory Hot-Dot option and overlapping tuner suppliers.'),res(guildHardwareSources.starfireFeatures,'Starfire IV Feature Chronology','Compare model-specific pickup, tuner and bridge changes; dates are approximate.'),historyResource,res(manuals,'Guild Electronics Manuals','Identify documented pickup and preamp systems on later acoustics.'),res(bluesbird,'Joe’s Bluesbird With An Added Bigsby','Compare a disclosed hardware change with the visible installation.')]},
{
slug:'guild-vintage-vs-reissue',label:'Vintage Vs. Reissue',title:'Vintage Guild Vs. Reissue: How To Tell Them Apart',metaTitle:'Vintage Guild Vs. Reissue: Labels, Serials & Factory Clues',description:'Separate Guild guitars from later models and reissues. Compare Westerly Collection labels, country markings, Newark St. names and replaced hardware.',intro:'Guild has reused model names and design details. Establish the production line and serial system before assigning an old date to a familiar-looking guitar.',photo:'d150Label',alt:'Westerly Collection label inside a later Guild D150NAT',
sections:[
{id:'westerly-name',title:'Westerly Factory Vs. Westerly Collection',html:`<p>A vintage Westerly Guild was built during the Rhode Island factory era. The modern Westerly Collection uses the name as a reference to that history. These meanings should remain separate in an identification or sale description.</p><p>Joe’s D-150NAT example makes the distinction visible. The model label says Westerly Collection, while its serial marking says Made in China. The guitar can carry Guild’s familiar name and styling without belonging to the old U.S. production sequence.</p><p>Read the full country and series wording before using the <a href="/guild-serial-number-lookup/">serial lookup</a>. A numeric substring inside a later serial should not be extracted and entered as if it were a complete 1960s number. The <a href="/guild-labels-and-serial-number-locations/#modern-country-markings">label guide</a> shows the two markings together.</p>`,photos:[pic('d150Label','Westerly Collection model label on a Guild D150NAT','The collection name recalls an earlier factory; it does not identify the place of manufacture.',d150),pic('d150Serial','Serial G1150733 and Made in China marking on a Guild D150NAT','The country marking supplies production context that the series name alone does not.',d150)]},
{id:'newark-street',title:'Newark St. And Revived Electric Designs',html:`<p>Later Newark St. instruments revisit Guild electric designs. A Starfire name, a vintage-style tailpiece or an old-looking logo is therefore insufficient evidence of a 1960s build. Read the complete model designation, country marking and serial before comparing the body with old photographs.</p><p>Guild’s <a href="${press}">current product announcements</a> describe reissued Starfire models with modern specifications, and an S-300 design that refers back to 1977. A year used to describe the original design is not the manufacture year of a newly made guitar.</p><p>The <a href="/guild-electric-guitar-identification/#starfire-family">Starfire identification guide</a> explains which construction and control details to record. Compare a specification for the exact version rather than counting how many vintage-looking features it carries.</p>`,photos:[pic('starfireFront','Single-cutaway Guild Starfire III from Joe’s photo archive','The Starfire family name spans multiple versions. This archive photograph alone is not a complete dating record.'),pic('starfireHead','Guild crown and vertical inlay on a Starfire headstock','Repeated design details need the serial and production context beside them.')]},
{id:'later-usa-guitars',title:'A Later USA Guild Can Also Look Vintage',html:`<p>Later U.S. Guild production passed through Corona, Tacoma, New Hartford and Oxnard. Familiar acoustic model names continue through different periods, with changes in factory, specifications and serial formats.</p><p>A Tacoma T-prefix or New Hartford N-prefix date code is read differently from an older model sequence. An Oxnard instrument should not be forced into that earlier Julian formula merely because it was made after 2005. The <a href="/guild-factory-eras/">factory-era guide</a> separates those systems and explains where the published coverage stops.</p><p>For an unresolved later guitar, save the original receipt, model suffix, serial and country marking. Compare the manufacturer's model information or request help with photographs. A confident-looking calculator result is not a substitute for a format that actually applies.</p>`,photos:[pic('f48Head','Guild crown and vertical headstock inlay on a vintage F48','A familiar Guild emblem can recur beyond one production period.'),pic('f48Label','F48 model and 74329 serial on a vintage Guild label','The complete model and numeric sequence establish this example’s context more clearly than the logo alone.')]},
{id:'modified-vintage-guilds',title:'A Vintage Guild Can Have Later Parts',html:`<p>A changed bridge, new tuners or replacement pickups don't turn a vintage body into a reissue. They change the instrument’s condition and originality description. Keep the build-date evidence and the repair history as separate parts of the record.</p><p>Joe’s black Bluesbird is a useful example: its <a href="${bluesbird}">listing discloses an added Bigsby</a>. A buyer who dated the entire guitar by that installed part could reach the wrong conclusion. Conversely, assuming a branded vibrato must be factory original would overlook a disclosed modification.</p><p>The <a href="/guild-pickup-and-hardware-dating-guide/">pickup and hardware guide</a> shows where to photograph mounting holes, tuner backs, bridge footprints and controls. Retain removed parts and receipts, but don't describe a loose part as original solely because it came in the case.</p>`,photos:[pic('bluesbirdFront','Vintage black Guild Bluesbird fitted with a later Bigsby','A disclosed hardware change belongs in the description without replacing the guitar’s separate dating evidence.',bluesbird),pic('bluesbirdBigsby','Close-up of the added Bigsby on Joe’s Guild Bluesbird','The listing explains the installation. A part’s visible branding does not establish originality.',bluesbird)]},
{id:'conflicting-evidence',title:'When The Number And Features Disagree',html:`<p>First check the transcription. A hidden letter, missed zero or partly visible stamp can change the chart. Next confirm the model and country. The 1960s charts reuse some prefixes, and later instruments can use strings that resemble earlier systems.</p><p>Then review known repairs and the limits of the records. Guild acknowledges gaps in its dating information. A missing entry is not proof that a guitar cannot exist, and a plausible entry does not prove an instrument is unmodified. Keep unresolved details visible instead of choosing the more desirable answer.</p><p>For a <a href="/free-appraisal/guild/">Guild appraisal</a>, send full front and back photographs, the label, both sides of the headstock and any disputed part. If you intend to <a href="/sell-my-guild-guitar/">sell the guitar</a>, a clear description of its model, date evidence and repairs makes the comparison with other instruments more useful.</p>`}
],resources:[res(collection,'Guild’s Westerly Collection Introduction','Read why the modern line uses a historical factory name.'),res(press,'Guild Reissue Announcements','Distinguish a revived design year from the actual manufacture date.'),faqResource]}
];
